Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.

${\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\,\text{d}x=$
{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$

First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:

$$
\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\frac{\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\,\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\frac{1}{\operatorname{sin}⁡x}
$$

Using Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are

$$
\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\,\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\frac{1}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.
$$

Now, we rewrite the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\operatorname{coth}⁡x\cdot \frac{1}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\,\text{d}x \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\cdot {sinh}^{2}⁡x\,\text{d}x \\  & ={\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x\end{aligned}
$$

We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then,

$$
\frac{\text{d}u}{\text{d}x}=\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,⟹\,\text{d}u=\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x.
$$

Now, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$

| $x$ | $0$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $u$ | $1$ | $\frac{5}{3}$ |

Now, we evaluate the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\begin{aligned}{\int }_{0}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{1}^{5/3}u\,\cdot \text{d}u \\  & =\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\  & =\frac{1}{2}({(\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\  & =\frac{1}{2}(\frac{25}{9}-1) \\  & =\frac{8}{9}\end{aligned}\end{aligned}
$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4af736d1-f000-5f15-b738-443b7520d5c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4af736d1-f000-5f15-b738-443b7520d5c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "4af736d1-f000-5f15-b738-443b7520d5c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2a2b669f-2579-5335-819c-e77eab944787" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2a2b669f-2579-5335-819c-e77eab944787"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a2b669f-2579-5335-819c-e77eab944787" :answer/id #uuid "2a2b669f-2579-5335-819c-e77eab944787" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{8}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7d070173-586a-5be7-bd5c-a4be4b18eeea" :question/id #uuid "7d070173-586a-5be7-bd5c-a4be4b18eeea" :question/math-academy-id "q-352966" :question/problem "$$

Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.

${\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡4}2\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\,\text{d}x=$
{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$

First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:

$$
\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\frac{\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\,\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\frac{1}{\operatorname{sin}⁡x}
$$

Using Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are

$$
\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\,\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\frac{1}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.
$$

Now, we rewrite the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡4}2\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\,\text{d}x & =2{\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\,\text{d}x \\  & =2{\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\frac{1}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)}\cdot \frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)}\,\text{d}x\end{aligned}
$$

We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then,

$$
\frac{\text{d}u}{\text{d}x}=2\,⟹\,\text{d}u=2\,\text{d}x.
$$

Now, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$

| $x$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $u$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡16$ |

Now, we evaluate the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\begin{aligned}2{\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡16}\operatorname{csch}⁡(u)\operatorname{coth}⁡(u)\,\cdot \text{d}u \\  & =-\operatorname{csch}⁡(u){|}_{\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡16} \\  & =-\operatorname{csch}⁡(\operatorname{ln}⁡16)+\operatorname{csch}⁡(\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\  & =-\frac{1}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\operatorname{ln}⁡16)}+\frac{1}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\operatorname{ln}⁡4)} \\  & =-\frac{1}{(\frac{16-{16}^{-1}}{2})}+\frac{1}{(\frac{4-{4}^{-1}}{2})} \\  & =-\frac{1}{(\frac{255}{32})}+\frac{1}{(\frac{15}{8})} \\  & =\frac{104}{255}\end{aligned}\end{aligned}
$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-59c3d70b-5195-5774-847f-b546cbff6162"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-59c3d70b-5195-5774-847f-b546cbff6162" :answer-field/id #uuid "59c3d70b-5195-5774-847f-b546cbff6162" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-036a64a0-313e-560e-a52e-dc6b8481224c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-036a64a0-313e-560e-a52e-dc6b8481224c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-036a64a0-313e-560e-a52e-dc6b8481224c" :answer/id #uuid "036a64a0-313e-560e-a52e-dc6b8481224c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{104}{255}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b4fa5f6e-c8a3-5731-a2a2-6fc871183cc3" :question/id #uuid "b4fa5f6e-c8a3-5731-a2a2-6fc871183cc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97309" :question/problem "$$

Evaluate ${\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{2}}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{3}}\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\,\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "$$

First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:

$$
\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\frac{\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.
$$

Using Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is

$$
\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.
$$

Now, we rewrite the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{2}}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{3}}\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{2}}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{3}}\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x}\,\text{d}x\end{aligned}
$$

We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.$ Then,

$$
\frac{\text{d}u}{\text{d}x}=2\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\,⟹\,\frac{1}{2}\,\text{d}u=\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\,\text{d}x.
$$

Now, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.$

| $x$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{2}$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{3}$ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $u$ | $\frac{3}{4}$ | $\frac{4}{3}$ |

Now, we evaluate the integral, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{2}}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡\sqrt{3}}\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x}\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\frac{1}{u}\cdot \frac{1}{2}\,\text{d}u \\  & =\frac{1}{2}{\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\frac{1}{u}\,\text{d}u \\  & =\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\  & =\frac{1}{2}(\operatorname{ln}⁡(\frac{4}{3})-\operatorname{ln}⁡(\frac{3}{4})) \\  & =\operatorname{ln}⁡(\frac{4}{3})\end{aligned}
$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4002135c-7d30-54e3-97cb-3c714ed2683f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4002135c-7d30-54e3-97cb-3c714ed2683f" :answer-field/id #uuid "4002135c-7d30-54e3-97cb-3c714ed2683f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4b6e5545-ad9f-555a-83ad-2208b3d8fb3f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d7c2265-44f1-5e96-9940-d8c787667e23" "answer-57ae105c-8d7c-5650-bab1-27e1f5494f13" "answer-62f42da6-b448-5558-a669-efbc711b9a8c" "answer-371ed2b5-3cfd-5f36-ad25-78277503bf11" "answer-4b6e5545-ad9f-555a-83ad-2208b3d8fb3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d7c2265-44f1-5e96-9940-d8c787667e23" :answer/id #uuid "7d7c2265-44f1-5e96-9940-d8c787667e23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{ln}⁡(\frac{8}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57ae105c-8d7c-5650-bab1-27e1f5494f13" :answer/id #uuid "57ae105c-8d7c-5650-bab1-27e1f5494f13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62f42da6-b448-5558-a669-efbc711b9a8c" :answer/id #uuid "62f42da6-b448-5558-a669-efbc711b9a8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-371ed2b5-3cfd-5f36-ad25-78277503bf11" :answer/id #uuid "371ed2b5-3cfd-5f36-ad25-78277503bf11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\operatorname{ln}⁡(\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b6e5545-ad9f-555a-83ad-2208b3d8fb3f" :answer/id #uuid "4b6e5545-ad9f-555a-83ad-2208b3d8fb3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\operatorname{ln}⁡(\frac{4}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8879f5ec-f2c2-51de-a1ef-edc8baad4b56" :question/id #uuid "8879f5ec-f2c2-51de-a1ef-edc8baad4b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-96498" :question/problem "$\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$

First, recall the following identity:

$$
\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\frac{1}{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}
$$

Then, we use the above identity and rewrite the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x & =\int \frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\cdot \operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x\end{aligned}
$$

We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then 

$$
\frac{\text{d}u}{\text{d}x}=\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,⟹\,\text{d}u=\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x.
$$

Using the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\int \frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\cdot \operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x & =\int \frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\,\text{d}u \\  & =-\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\  & =-\frac{1}{3{cosh}^{3}⁡x}+C \\  & =-\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C\end{aligned}
$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d71d4fa5-c4c0-5c1e-bcc7-f6ccd2a9c51a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d71d4fa5-c4c0-5c1e-bcc7-f6ccd2a9c51a" :answer-field/id #uuid "d71d4fa5-c4c0-5c1e-bcc7-f6ccd2a9c51a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec738ce0-bbfd-546f-8829-537d42455730" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3076c98e-4b5f-5295-a960-def6c68868a2" "answer-ec738ce0-bbfd-546f-8829-537d42455730" "answer-d05d1a37-cbd0-5beb-a2c1-20973dce818c" "answer-951bf2e0-4ba8-5e1e-9dc3-e53cb361313b" "answer-790e9e20-c3f7-5319-bd5c-6ff1a8086450"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3076c98e-4b5f-5295-a960-def6c68868a2" :answer/id #uuid "3076c98e-4b5f-5295-a960-def6c68868a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec738ce0-bbfd-546f-8829-537d42455730" :answer/id #uuid "ec738ce0-bbfd-546f-8829-537d42455730" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d05d1a37-cbd0-5beb-a2c1-20973dce818c" :answer/id #uuid "d05d1a37-cbd0-5beb-a2c1-20973dce818c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-951bf2e0-4ba8-5e1e-9dc3-e53cb361313b" :answer/id #uuid "951bf2e0-4ba8-5e1e-9dc3-e53cb361313b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-790e9e20-c3f7-5319-bd5c-6ff1a8086450" :answer/id #uuid "790e9e20-c3f7-5319-bd5c-6ff1a8086450" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-390d417d-9926-5446-a159-d0e16ff521f1" :question/id #uuid "390d417d-9926-5446-a159-d0e16ff521f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-138954" :question/problem "$$

${\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\,\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$

First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:

$$
\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\frac{\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\,\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\frac{1}{\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.
$$

Using Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are

$$
\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\,\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\frac{1}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.
$$

Now, we rewrite the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\cdot {(\frac{1}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\text{d}x \\  & ={\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\cdot \frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\,\text{d}x \\  & ={\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\,\text{d}x\end{aligned}
$$

We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,

$$
\frac{\text{d}u}{\text{d}x}=\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\,⟹\,\text{d}u=\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\,\text{d}x.
$$

Now, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$

| $x$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $u$ | $\frac{3}{4}$ | $\frac{4}{3}$ |

Now, we evaluate the integral as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\,\cdot \text{d}u \\  & ={\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\,\cdot \text{d}u \\  & =\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\  & =-\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\  & =-\frac{1}{2{(\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\frac{1}{2{(\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\  & =-\frac{9}{32}+\frac{8}{9} \\  & =\frac{175}{288}\end{aligned}
$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7e082909-a6e2-55c2-a4a7-4878e06b14ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7e082909-a6e2-55c2-a4a7-4878e06b14ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e082909-a6e2-55c2-a4a7-4878e06b14ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-575e949f-a2b7-5302-ade7-234c593749f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-575e949f-a2b7-5302-ade7-234c593749f2" "answer-84f09ef6-af8c-5278-868c-878e38fd43f7" "answer-e4be4b9f-6f9d-51ae-b85e-355d75cde324" "answer-94da350d-d600-5d09-a5e8-a507d6bea83a" "answer-9f8049a3-094e-56a7-a9bb-cd14ccc9b2ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-575e949f-a2b7-5302-ade7-234c593749f2" :answer/id #uuid "575e949f-a2b7-5302-ade7-234c593749f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{175}{288}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84f09ef6-af8c-5278-868c-878e38fd43f7" :answer/id #uuid "84f09ef6-af8c-5278-868c-878e38fd43f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{175}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4be4b9f-6f9d-51ae-b85e-355d75cde324" :answer/id #uuid "e4be4b9f-6f9d-51ae-b85e-355d75cde324" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{243}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94da350d-d600-5d09-a5e8-a507d6bea83a" :answer/id #uuid "94da350d-d600-5d09-a5e8-a507d6bea83a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{35}{288}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f8049a3-094e-56a7-a9bb-cd14ccc9b2ae" :answer/id #uuid "9f8049a3-094e-56a7-a9bb-cd14ccc9b2ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\frac{35}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168117 :tutorial/content "It's possible to convert every trigonometric identity to a corresponding *hyperbolic* identity using **Osborn's rule.** 

To apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:

1\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.

2\. For any product of *two trigonometric sines*, change the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.

To demonstrate, consider the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:

$$
\newcommand{\sech}{\mathop{\rm sech}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\csch}{\mathop{\rm csch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\coth}{\mathop{\rm coth}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arsinh}{\mathop{\rm arsinh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcosh}{\mathop{\rm arcosh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\artanh}{\mathop{\rm artanh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arsech}{\mathop{\rm arsech}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcsch}{\mathop{\rm arcsch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcoth}{\mathop{\rm arcoth}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\cosh}{\mathop{\rm cosh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\sinh}{\mathop{\rm sinh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\csch}{\mathop{\rm csch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\coth}{\mathop{\rm coth}\nolimits}
\cos^2 x \:{\color{blue}{\boldsymbol +}}\: \sin^2 x = 1
$$

Notice that this trigonometric identity contains a product of two sines. Therefore, to find the corresponding hyperbolic identity, we change each trigonometric function to its corresponding hyperbolic counterpart and change the sign in front of the corresponding product of sines. 

Thus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is

$$
\cosh^2 x \:{\color{red}{\boldsymbol -}}\: \sinh^2 x = 1.
$$



We can check that this identity is indeed true using the definitions of the hyperbolic sine and cosine:

$$
\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x & ={(\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))}^{2}-{(\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}))}^{2} \\  & =\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2} \\  & =\frac{1}{4}({({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2}) \\  & =\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x}-({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})) \\  & =\frac{1}{4}(\cancel{{e}^{2x}}+2+\cancel{{e}^{-2x}}-(\cancel{{e}^{2x}}-2+\cancel{{e}^{-2x}})) \\  & =\frac{1}{4}\cdot 4 \\  & =1\,✓\end{aligned}
$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168153 :tutorial/content "When applying Osborn's rule, we must be aware that sometimes a product of two sines is "hidden."

To demonstrate, consider the following trigonometric identity:

$$
\newcommand{\sech}{\mathop{\rm sech}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\csch}{\mathop{\rm csch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\coth}{\mathop{\rm coth}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arsinh}{\mathop{\rm arsinh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcosh}{\mathop{\rm arcosh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\artanh}{\mathop{\rm artanh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arsech}{\mathop{\rm arsech}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcsch}{\mathop{\rm arcsch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcoth}{\mathop{\rm arcoth}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\cosh}{\mathop{\rm cosh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\sinh}{\mathop{\rm sinh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\csch}{\mathop{\rm csch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\coth}{\mathop{\rm coth}\nolimits}
1 \:{\color{blue}{\boldsymbol +}}\: \tan^2 x = \sec^2 x
$$

At first glance, it may seem that there is no product of sines, and we do not need to change any signs in the corresponding hyperbolic identity.

However, recall that

$$
\tan x = \dfrac{\sin x}{\cos x}
$$

which implies that

$$
\tan^2 x = \dfrac{\sin^2 x}{\cos^2 x}.
$$

So, there is a hidden product of sines within the $\tan^2 x$ term, and we need to flip the sign in front of this term when writing down the corresponding hyperbolic identity.

Thus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is

$$
1 \:{\color{red}{\boldsymbol -}}\: \tanh^2 x = \sech^2 x.
$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168152 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral

$$
\newcommand{\sech}{\mathop{\rm sech}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\csch}{\mathop{\rm csch}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\coth}{\mathop{\rm coth}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arsinh}{\mathop{\rm arsinh}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arcosh}{\mathop{\rm arcosh}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\artanh}{\mathop{\rm artanh}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arsech}{\mathop{\rm arsech}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arcsch}{\mathop{\rm arcsch}\nolimits}
        \newcommand{\arcoth}{\mathop{\rm arcoth}\nolimits}

\int \dfrac{1}{\sinh^2 x}\,\textrm{d}x.
$$

First, we note the following hyperbolic identity:

$$
\csch{x} = \dfrac{1}{\sinh{x}}
$$

Therefore, we can write our integral as

$$
\int \dfrac{1}{\sinh^2{x}} \,\textrm{d}x
= \int \left(\dfrac{1}{\sinh{x}}\right)^2 \,\textrm{d}x
= \int \csch^2{x} \,\textrm{d}x.
$$

From here, we can see that this is just a standard hyperbolic integral:

