{{field-2}} days."] ["question-d80dc7c0-9327-55d2-9040-e9261d08f05c" :question/worked-solution "To find when the total amount is a minimum, we need to solve $\frac{\text{d}M}{\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{\text{d}M}{\text{d}t} & =\frac{\text{d}}{\text{d}t}(1500+{\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-27)\,\text{d}x) \\  & =\frac{\text{d}}{\text{d}t}(1500)+\frac{\text{d}}{\text{d}t}({\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-27)\,\text{d}x) \\  & =\frac{\text{d}}{\text{d}t}{\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-27)\,\text{d}x \\  & =3{t}^{2}-27\end{aligned}
$$



Now, we solve $\frac{\text{d}M}{\text{d}t}=0$:

$$
3{t}^{2}-27=0\,⇒\,t=3
$$

So there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how much money is in the account when $t=3,$ we figure out $M(3)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}M(3) & =1500+{\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-27)\,\text{d}x \\  & =1500+[{x}^{3}-27x]{|}_{0}^{3} \\  & =1500+[({3}^{3}-27(3))-(0)] \\  & =1500+[27-81] \\  & =1500-54 \\  & =1446\end{aligned}
$$

Since $1446<1500$, we conclude that the minimum amount is $$\,1446$, and this will happen after $3$ days."] ["question-d80dc7c0-9327-55d2-9040-e9261d08f05c" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-a36923dd-8b5c-56f5-b238-c554200e0681" :answer-field/id #uuid "a36923dd-8b5c-56f5-b238-c554200e0681"] ["answer-df50d26b-36b0-56c5-b8ab-6313ce61f333" :answer/id #uuid "df50d26b-36b0-56c5-b8ab-6313ce61f333"] ["answer-df50d26b-36b0-56c5-b8ab-6313ce61f333" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-df50d26b-36b0-56c5-b8ab-6313ce61f333" :answer/value "1446"] ["answer-field-a36923dd-8b5c-56f5-b238-c554200e0681" :answer-field/key "field-1"] ["answer-field-a36923dd-8b5c-56f5-b238-c554200e0681" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-a36923dd-8b5c-56f5-b238-c554200e0681" :answer-field/correct "answer-df50d26b-36b0-56c5-b8ab-6313ce61f333"] ["answer-field-a36923dd-8b5c-56f5-b238-c554200e0681" :answer-field/choices "answer-df50d26b-36b0-56c5-b8ab-6313ce61f333"] ["answer-field-b20711af-5c25-5a71-ae61-9921ee570e17" :answer-field/id #uuid "b20711af-5c25-5a71-ae61-9921ee570e17"] ["answer-15462584-19cb-5b6e-9995-3d3c0d41935d" :answer/id #uuid "15462584-19cb-5b6e-9995-3d3c0d41935d"] ["answer-15462584-19cb-5b6e-9995-3d3c0d41935d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-15462584-19cb-5b6e-9995-3d3c0d41935d" :answer/value "3"] ["answer-field-b20711af-5c25-5a71-ae61-9921ee570e17" :answer-field/key "field-2"] ["answer-field-b20711af-5c25-5a71-ae61-9921ee570e17" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-b20711af-5c25-5a71-ae61-9921ee570e17" :answer-field/correct "answer-15462584-19cb-5b6e-9995-3d3c0d41935d"] ["answer-field-b20711af-5c25-5a71-ae61-9921ee570e17" :answer-field/choices "answer-15462584-19cb-5b6e-9995-3d3c0d41935d"] ["question-d80dc7c0-9327-55d2-9040-e9261d08f05c" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-a36923dd-8b5c-56f5-b238-c554200e0681"] ["question-d80dc7c0-9327-55d2-9040-e9261d08f05c" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-b20711af-5c25-5a71-ae61-9921ee570e17"] [17592186147399 :knowledge-point/questions "question-d80dc7c0-9327-55d2-9040-e9261d08f05c"] [17592186176241 :question/problem "![](images/58/5806f57e41eeb5548b0c041533adf3952dd7f35236121f9056c57f03c2854ddc.png)

Consider the arc of the curve $y=\sqrt{1-x}$ that lies in the first quadrant between $x=0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?"] [17592186176241 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula

$$
S=\int 2\pi x\,\text{d}s.
$$

Since we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have 

$$
\text{d}s=\sqrt{1+{(\frac{\text{d}y}{\text{d}x})}^{2}}\,\text{d}x.
$$

Therefore, we will use

$$
S=2\pi {\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\sqrt{1+{(\frac{\text{d}y}{\text{d}x})}^{2}}\,\text{d}x.
$$

First, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:

$$
\begin{aligned}\sqrt{1-x} & =0 \\ 1-x & =0 \\ x & =1\end{aligned}
$$

Hence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$ 

Computing the derivative, we get

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{\text{d}y}{\text{d}x} & =\frac{\text{d}}{\text{d}x}(\sqrt{1-x})=-\frac{1}{2\sqrt{1-x}}.\end{aligned}
$$

Finally, we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}S & =2\pi {\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\sqrt{1+{(\frac{\text{d}y}{\text{d}x})}^{2}}\,\text{d}x \\  & =2\pi {\int }_{0}^{1}x\sqrt{1+{(-\frac{1}{2\sqrt{1-x}})}^{2}}\,\text{d}x \\  & =2\pi {\int }_{0}^{1}x\sqrt{1+\frac{1}{4(1-x)}}\,\text{d}x \\  & =2\pi {\int }_{0}^{1}x\sqrt{\frac{5-4x}{4(1-x)}}\,\text{d}x \\  & =\pi {\int }_{0}^{1}x\sqrt{\frac{4x-5}{x-1}}\,\text{d}x.\end{aligned}
